  "solutions": [
    {"ordinal": 0, "title": "Sort, Then Sweep", "intuition_md": "Unsorted, any booking could overlap any other, so deciding what to merge means comparing pairs. Sorted by `start`, the question collapses to a local one: a booking can only extend the busy period *most recently opened*. Every earlier period ended before that one began, and every later booking starts no earlier than this one.\n\nSo sweep left to right, holding the current busy period. A booking that starts at or before its end (`<=`, because touching windows leave no gap) stretches it; anything else closes it and opens a new one.", "algorithm_md": "1. Sort the bookings by `start` (pairs compare by their first element first, so a plain sort of the pairs does it).\n2. For each `(start, end)`: if there's a last busy period and `start <= last.end`, set `last.end = max(last.end, end)`. The `max` matters for a booking nested inside the current period. Otherwise open a new period `(start, end)`.\n3. Return the busy periods.", "code": {"python": "def merge_bookings(bookings: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    busy: list[list[int]] = []\n    for start, end in sorted(bookings):\n        # Overlapping or touching: [a, b) and [b, c) leave no idle gap.\n        if busy and start <= busy[-1][1]:\n            busy[-1][1] = max(busy[-1][1], end)\n        else:\n            busy.append([start, end])\n    return busy\n", "js": "var mergeBookings = function(bookings) {\n    // Copy before sorting: sort() reorders in place, and the input isn't ours.\n    var sorted = bookings.slice().sort(function(a, b) { return a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]; });\n    var busy = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < sorted.length; i++) {\n        var start = sorted[i][0], end = sorted[i][1];\n        var last = busy[busy.length - 1];\n        // Overlapping or touching: [a, b) and [b, c) leave no idle gap.\n        if (last && start <= last[1]) {\n            last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end);\n        } else {\n            busy.push([start, end]);\n        }\n    }\n    return busy;\n};\n", "rust": "fn merge_bookings(mut bookings: Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {\n    bookings.sort_unstable();\n    let mut busy: Vec<(i64, i64)> = Vec::with_capacity(bookings.len());\n    for (start, end) in bookings {\n        match busy.last_mut() {\n            // Overlapping or touching: [a, b) and [b, c) leave no idle gap.\n            Some(last) if start <= last.1 => last.1 = last.1.max(end),\n            _ => busy.push((start, end)),\n        }\n    }\n    busy\n}\n"}, "time_complexity": "O(n \\log n)", "time_complexity_reason": "The sort dominates. The sweep touches each booking once and does constant work for it.", "space_complexity": "O(n)", "space_complexity_reason": "The output holds up to $n$ periods when nothing overlaps. Python's `sorted` and the JavaScript copy add another $O(n)$; Rust sorts the owned `Vec` in place with `sort_unstable`, needing only $O(\\log n)$ of stack."},
    {"ordinal": 1, "title": "Compact in Place", "intuition_md": "The sweep never looks back further than the last busy period, and the number of busy periods written so far never exceeds the number of bookings read. So the output can overwrite the input as it goes: a write cursor `w` trails the read cursor `r`, and `bookings[..w]` is always the finished answer so far.\n\nThis is the same algorithm as the sweep, with the second allocation removed. It's a Rust idiom when a function owns its input `Vec`, and the same shape as `Vec::dedup_by`.", "algorithm_md": "1. Sort as before. Set `w = 0`.\n2. For each index `r`, read `(start, end) = bookings[r]`. If `w > 0` and `start <= bookings[w - 1].1`, extend `bookings[w - 1]`. Otherwise write the booking to `bookings[w]` and increment `w`. Since `w <= r`, the write never clobbers a booking that hasn't been read yet.\n3. `truncate(w)` and return the same `Vec`.", "code": {"rust": "fn merge_bookings(mut bookings: Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {\n    bookings.sort_unstable();\n    // `w` counts the finished busy periods, kept in bookings[..w]. Each booking\n    // either stretches the last one or becomes the next, so the Vec is reused\n    // as its own output and no second allocation is needed.\n    let mut w = 0;\n    for r in 0..bookings.len() {\n        let (start, end) = bookings[r];\n        if w > 0 && start <= bookings[w - 1].1 {\n            bookings[w - 1].1 = bookings[w - 1].1.max(end);\n        } else {\n            bookings[w] = (start, end);\n            w += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    bookings.truncate(w);\n    bookings\n}\n"}, "time_complexity": "O(n \\log n)", "time_complexity_reason": "Identical to the sweep: sorting, then one linear pass.", "space_complexity": "O(\\log n)", "space_complexity_reason": "No second `Vec`, because the answer is built inside the input's own buffer. What remains is the sort's recursion stack."}
  ]
